p1_add = """ In enterprise software architecture, one of the most critical design choices is deciding between an Abstract Class and an Interface. An Abstract Class is a class marked with the 'abstract' keyword that cannot be directly instantiated with 'new'. Unlike an interface, an abstract class CAN declare mutable instance fields, maintain constructor initialization logic, and define private or protected helper methods. However, because an abstract class is still a class, subclasses can extend only ONE abstract class due to single inheritance. An Interface, in contrast, cannot declare instance fields or constructors, but a class can implement unlimited numbers of interfaces. In Joshua Bloch's Effective Java (Item 20), the recommended architectural standard is: 'Prefer interfaces to abstract classes', while combining them through Skeletal Implementations (Abstract Skeletal Classes like AbstractList or AbstractMap) where an interface defines the public contract and an abstract class provides default boilerplate logic. Furthermore, at the HotSpot execution layer, invoking methods via 'invokeinterface' historically required scanning the class's ITable; however, modern HotSpot C2 compiler applies sophisticated Monomorphic Inline Caching to ITables as well, reducing interface calls to direct, single-cycle branch instructions when only a single implementation is active at runtime."""

p2_add = """ Another crucial dimension of contract realization is the prevention of the Constant Interface Anti-Pattern. In early versions of Java before enums existed, some developers created interfaces containing only constants (e.g. 'interface PhysicsConstants { double GRAVITY = 9.81; }') and had classes implement them just to access the variable names without typing the class prefix. Effective Java (Item 22) formally condemned this practice as a severe architectural defect: implementing an interface is a public statement about what the class's capabilities are; leaking internal implementation constants into a class's exported API violates encapsulation and creates permanent backward-compatibility debt. In modern Java, constants should be declared inside dedicated final utility classes with private constructors or Java Enums. Additionally, when a concrete class implements an interface, it must rigorously honor all method contracts: if an interface method specifies that returning 'false' indicates payment rejection, the implementing class cannot deviate by returning 'true' or throwing unhandled runtime exceptions for standard negative inputs, preserving behavioral substitutability across all client modules."""

p3_add = """ In modern enterprise computing\u2014spanning financial cloud microservices, payment gateways, and banking APIs\u2014Dependency Inversion is operationalized through Inversion of Control (IoC) Containers such as the Spring Framework and Google Guice. In these enterprise platforms, services never instantiate their dependencies directly using 'new'. Instead, the IoC container reads configuration metadata, discovers classes decorated with '@Component' or '@Service' that implement the target interface, instantiates them as singleton beans in memory, and injects them into consuming services automatically via Constructor Injection. In automated CI/CD testing pipelines, dependency inversion allows mocking frameworks (such as Mockito) to generate Dynamic Bytecode Proxies on the fly: Mockito synthesizes an in-memory class that implements 'PaymentGateway' in a fraction of a millisecond, stubbing 'when(mockGateway.processPayment(100.0)).thenReturn(true);'. This allows comprehensive regression suites to test edge cases, error conditions, and network failures with lightning speed and 100% determinism without requiring physical database servers or live payment networks."""

p4_add = """ The real-world brilliance of Java 8 Default Methods is best illustrated by the evolution of the Java Collections Framework. Prior to Java 8, adding a new functional capability like '.stream()' or '.forEach()' to the core 'java.util.Collection' interface would have been impossible without breaking every existing database driver, collection library, and custom list class worldwide. By defining 'default Stream<E> stream() { return StreamSupport.stream(spliterator(), false); }', the Java platform architects retrofitted streaming and functional programming onto hundreds of existing collection classes instantaneously! However, the JVM enforces one fundamental rule regarding default methods: The Class Always Wins Rule! If a superclass declares a concrete method with the same signature as an interface default method, the superclass method ALWAYS takes precedence, and the interface default method is completely ignored. Furthermore, default methods are strictly prohibited from overriding Object's methods ('equals()', 'hashCode()', 'toString()') because every Java object already inherits concrete implementations from java.lang.Object, so an interface default would never be able to execute under the class-always-wins rule."""

p5_add = """ In our automated test harness implemented within the main method, we execute comprehensive verification suites testing every dimension of Milestone 4 contract integrity:
- Phase 1 (Valid Positive Payment Execution): Asserts that 'Solution.execute(100.0)' executes through the decoupled PaymentGateway interface reference and evaluates to 'true', confirming that valid positive transactions are certified.
- Phase 2 (Boundary Zero Payment Interception): Asserts that 'Solution.execute(0.0)' evaluates to 'false', verifying that zero-dollar transactions are cleanly intercepted and rejected.
- Phase 3 (Negative Payment Interception): Asserts that 'Solution.execute(-50.0)' evaluates to 'false', validating that negative amounts cannot drain ledger balances.
- Phase 4 (Assignment Contract Verification): Asserts that 'Solution.test()' evaluates to 'Document printed' for a Document instance implementing the Printable interface.
- Phase 5 (Type Compatibility & Upcasting Assertions): Asserts via 'instanceof' that CryptoGateway satisfies both its concrete class type and the PaymentGateway interface contract.
This complete verification harness confirms that your Milestone 4 payment engine achieves 100% compliance with decoupled enterprise standards, certifying your mastery over Java interfaces and contract-driven architecture."""

